                                "sense_id": "0aea0e18-c5db-33f0-a9be-cbacfd28376b",
                                "text": "1984, D. E. Harder, 11: Developmental Ultrastructure of Hyphae and Spores, William Bushnell, Alan P. Roelfs (editors), The Cereal Rusts: Origins, Volume I: Specificity, Structure, and Physiology, page 355,\nIn the inner wall facing the aecial cavity, the primary wall materials surrounding the processes disintegrate, partially exposing them."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "06b6908b-df0d-4939-a7f1-5a88b78fda57",
                                "sense_id": "0aea0e18-c5db-33f0-a9be-cbacfd28376b",
                                "text": "Aecial hosts are primarily conifers. Damage to some aecial hosts may also be considered important, as in pine twist rust caused by M.^([Melampsora]) pinitorqua Rostrup."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e2f0f376-2166-4505-b9f1-118bb19d35d9",
                                "sense_id": "0aea0e18-c5db-33f0-a9be-cbacfd28376b",
                                "text": "The uredo- and teleuto-stages occur on wheat, barley and some grasses and the pycnidial and aecial stages on species of Berberis and Mahonia, the alternate hosts."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
